1 ANSWER
  Picks up the call ringing your phone, and adds a viewport for the
  caller below yours.

    ANSWER

  If no one is calling, PHONE says so.
1 Characters
  In a call, each character you type goes to everyone you are talking
  with as you type it, and shows in your own viewport. These keys edit
  the line being typed, in every viewport:

    RETURN    starts a new line
    DELETE    rubs out the last character
    LINEFEED  rubs out the last word
    CTRL/U    rubs out the line
    CTRL/L    clears the viewport
    TAB       moves to the next tab stop
    CTRL/G    rings the bell
1 Control_keys
  Keys that PHONE itself takes:

    CTRL/Z    hangs up a call; while a command is typed, or with no
              one on the line, hangs up and leaves PHONE
    CTRL/W    draws the screen again
    CTRL/U    while a command is typed, rubs it out

  Any key cancels the calls still ringing, and ends a directory
  listing.
1 DIAL
  Rings another user of this system, every 10 seconds until the user
  answers or rejects the call, or you press a key.

    DIAL username

  With no one on the line, typing just the username does the same.
  PHONE is an abbreviation for DIAL.

  The user must be logged in at a terminal that takes broadcasts.
  vaxpunk has no network: a node name, as in NODE::USER, is refused.
1 DIRECTORY
  Lists the users logged in at terminals on this system, and whether
  each one's phone is available or unplugged (/NOBROADCAST).

    DIRECTORY

  Press any key to end the listing.
1 EXIT
  Hangs up on everyone and leaves PHONE.

    EXIT

  CTRL/Z does the same when no one is on the line.
1 HANGUP
  Ends the call with everyone you are talking with.

    HANGUP

  CTRL/Z does the same during a call.
1 HELP
  Describes PHONE's commands.

    HELP [topic]
1 REJECT
  Turns down the call ringing your phone. The caller is told.

    REJECT
1 Switch_hook
  During a call, the switch hook character, % unless the PHONE command's
  /SWITCH_HOOK qualifier picks another, starts a command: type it, then
  the command, then RETURN. With no one on the line, you can type the
  command without it.

  A command may be abbreviated: the abbreviation picks the first
  command, in alphabetical order, that starts with it.
